                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e7008005-e989-34c3-b031-6673d0f645e3",
                        "entry_id": "f7a732e7-beab-35a2-bc8c-a94228ae2998",
                        "glosses": [
                            "sternförmige Anordnung der Chromosomen an der Äquatorialplatte vor der Chromosomenspaltung in der Metaphase der Mitose"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "62bd14b2-eb6a-4507-8667-c78a116fd229",
                                "sense_id": "e7008005-e989-34c3-b031-6673d0f645e3",
                                "text": "„Bei den chromosomalen Mikrotubuli unterschiedet man: Microtubuli continui und Mikrotubuli chromosomales. Letztere bewirken Bildung des Monasters, später Trennung und – durch abbaubedingte Verkürzung – das in der Anaphase erfolgende Auseinanderweichen der Tochterchromosomen bzw. Chromatiden. Spindelgifte (z.B. Colchicin) verhindern die Zellteilung.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "19c0b7c1-66e9-496f-aa3e-d916d4019b0d",
                                "sense_id": "e7008005-e989-34c3-b031-6673d0f645e3",
                                "text": "„Nachdem die Monaster gebildet wurden, teilen sich die Chromosomen in zwei genau gleiche Hälften.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8db51566-ab8e-4af6-8213-710516b0766b",
                                "sense_id": "e7008005-e989-34c3-b031-6673d0f645e3",
                                "text": "„Betrachtet man die Monaster seitlich (Abb. 17 und 20), so sieht man ebenfalls die großen Chromosomen als lange, fingerförmige Fortsätze herausragen.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6a3145c5-cc32-48b5-8459-24eefdc89106",
                        "source_entry_id": "f7a732e7-beab-35a2-bc8c-a94228ae2998",
                        "source_sense_id": "e7008005-e989-34c3-b031-6673d0f645e3",
                        "target_lang_word": "monaster",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "monaster",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                        "entry_id": "55451de5-c7ec-3c51-806d-9d38ddb0ae3a",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The single aster formation that forms at the equatorial plate at the end of metaphase in mitosis."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fd733d3e-e90e-4a40-a7ee-45b9f7eff2e8",
                                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                                "text": "After the encounter of the two pronuclei, one quadrant of the former positive cross of the monaster is missing on the side of the egg pronucleus."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bfabbf83-a8ab-4ae3-b4ae-32450c21e494",
                                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                                "text": "One finds the longest chromosomes in the vascular epithelial cells if the monaster is well developed (Fig. 24), whilst shortly beforehand (Fig. 15) the chromosomes are still rather short."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ab85600e-3f48-4c4a-b6c8-e1eea83916ee",
                                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                                "text": "At the same time the middle piece breaks down and releases the centriole, around which egg cytoplasm forms the sperm monaster."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f36733c7-fe71-4bee-9ddc-5415ead8dcf5",
                                                "sense_id": "026b638e-39c8-3783-a584-5aab4a46445f",
                                                "text": "Large monasters can be produced in egg cells by a variety of methods; drugs, such as strychnine or chloral hydrate (Hertwig, 1887 ), hypertonic sea water (Morgan, 1896; Wilson,1901;Herlant, 1918),and by mechanical agitation (Boveri, 1903; Painter, 1915)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
